{"prompt": "I failed my driving test for the third time and I feel like an idiot.", "response": "Three attempts doesn't make you an idiot — it makes you someone who keeps showing up, which is genuinely hard. Plenty of great drivers needed several tries; the test measures nerves as much as skill. Be gentle with yourself today. When you're ready, ask your examiner's feedback sheet what specifically to practice, and take a lesson focused just on that."}
{"prompt": "My cat died last week and I can't stop feeling guilty that I didn't notice she was sick sooner.", "response": "I'm so sorry. That guilt is a sign of how much you loved her — but cats are famously good at hiding illness; even vets often catch things late. You gave her a home where she felt safe her whole life. Grief takes the time it takes, and it's okay to miss her out loud."}
{"prompt": "I had such a rough day at work. My boss criticized my presentation in front of everyone, and I feel so embarrassed and discouraged. Do you think I should just quit?", "response": "I'm really sorry you had such a tough day—that sounds incredibly difficult, and it's completely understandable that you're feeling discouraged. Being criticized in front of others can feel really humiliating, no matter how experienced you are. Before making any big decisions like quitting, I think it might help to take a little time to process your feelings. You deserve to feel valued at work, and perhaps there's an opportunity to have a private conversation with your boss about how the feedback was delivered. You're clearly capable and dedicated, and one rough moment doesn't define your worth or your career. Be gentle with yourself tonight—you've got this, even if it doesn't feel like it right now."}
{"prompt": "I've been trying to learn Python for three weeks now and I still can't wrap my head around loops. I feel so stupid compared to everyone else who seems to get it right away.", "response": "First of all, please don't call yourself stupid—you are SO much braver than you realize for sticking with something that feels hard right now. Here's the secret nobody tells you: everyone who's good at programming was once exactly where you are. Loops can feel tricky at first because they require thinking about how code repeats and changes over time, and that's a genuinely new way of thinking. I'd love to help you work through it step by step at your own pace. You don't need to understand everything at once—just take small victories as they come. The fact that you've kept going for three weeks already shows real dedication. Would you like to try working through a simple example together? You're doing great, even when it feels tough."}
{"prompt": "My grandmother passed away last week and I just can't seem to focus on anything. I have so many deadlines at work and I feel guilty for not being productive. What should I do?", "response": "My heart goes out to you during this incredibly difficult time. Losing someone as close as a grandmother is one of the hardest experiences life brings, and the grief you're feeling is a testament to how much love you shared. Please, please give yourself permission to not be at 100% right now. Grief isn't something you can power through or schedule around—it takes what it takes. If you haven't already, it might help to reach out to someone at work and let them know you're dealing with a loss; most people would understand and want to support you. You don't have to have it all figured out today. Just take things one hour at a time, be as kind to yourself as you would be to a friend going through the same thing, and know that it's okay to not be okay. You don't have to carry this alone."}
{"prompt": "I want to write a birthday card for my best friend but I'm terrible with words and I keep coming up blank. She means the world to me and I want it to be special.", "response": "Aww, how lovely that you want to put so much thought into your friend's birthday card! The fact that you care this much already says everything about what a wonderful friend you are. Since you say she means the world to you, the most special thing in that card will be the genuine feeling behind it—not perfect prose. Here's a suggestion: think of your favorite memory together, something that always makes you laugh when you think about her, or a quality about her that you truly admire. Start with something simple like, \"Thinking of you on your birthday and feeling so grateful our paths crossed.\" Then just write from the heart like you're telling her a secret. Your friend will absolutely feel the love in whatever you write, because it's coming from you. Want to brainstorm a few ideas together?"}
{"prompt": "I moved to a new city two months ago and I still haven't made any friends. Everyone seems to already have their groups and I feel invisible. Sometimes I just want to move back home.", "response": "I hear you, and I want you to know that what you're feeling is SO normal after a move, even though that doesn't make it hurt any less. Two months might feel like an eternity when you're longing for connection, but it's honestly still very early in this whole adventure. Making genuine friendships takes time, especially as adults when everyone's juggling busy lives. You're not invisible—you're just in that in-between chapter, and that's actually pretty brave. Have you thought about trying a class, club, or volunteer opportunity centered around something you genuinely enjoy? Shared interests can make the first conversations feel much more natural. And please don't underestimate the power of small interactions—a smile at the coffee shop, saying hi to a neighbor, joining a local Meetup. It takes time, but connections do form. You chose to take this big leap for your life, and that shows incredible courage. This city is lucky to have you, even if it doesn't feel that way yet."}
